          "distinct": true,
          "id": "afdfeaf162899f3625c15c4c90f4b20f526d3128",
          "message": "fix: macOS-14 .NET build architecture to ARM64 (#4726)\n\n* Fix macOS-14 .NET build architecture to ARM64\n\nmacOS-14 runners use Apple Silicon (ARM64), but the workflow was\nconfigured to build for osx-x64 (Intel), causing .NET runtime failures.\n\nChanges:\n- Changed macos-14 runtime_id from osx-x64 to osx-arm64\n- Updated test jobs to download osx-arm64 runtime artifacts\n- macos-15-intel remains osx-x64 (correct for Intel runners)\n\nFixes the .NET location errors seen in CI:\n  Download the .NET runtime:\n  https://aka.ms/dotnet-core-applaunch?missing_runtime=true&arch=arm64...\n\n* Fix hardcoded osx-x64 in C# NuGet build configuration\n\nThe CMakeLists.txt was hardcoding NUGET_RUNTIME_ID to osx-x64 for all\nmacOS builds, but it needs to be osx-arm64 when building on ARM64.\n\nNow detects architecture using CMAKE_SYSTEM_PROCESSOR:\n- ARM64/AArch64 → osx-arm64\n- Other (Intel) → osx-x64\n\nThis complements the workflow fix to fully support macOS-14 ARM64 builds.\n\n* Add .NET SDK setup before installing dotnet tools\n\nThe workflow was attempting to install dotnet-t4 without first\nensuring .NET SDK is installed. On macOS-14 ARM64 runners, this\ncauses failures as the SDK may not be pre-installed or may be\nthe wrong architecture.\n\nAdded actions/setup-dotnet@v3 to install .NET 8.0 SDK before\ninstalling global tools.\n\n* Remove hardcoded x64 PlatformTarget from NuGet test projects\n\nThe test projects had PlatformTarget hardcoded to x64, which caused\nbuild failures when testing ARM64 packages:\n\n  error NETSDK1032: The RuntimeIdentifier platform 'osx-arm64' and\n  the PlatformTarget 'x64' must be compatible.\n\nRemoved the hardcoded PlatformTarget to allow it to be inferred from\nthe RuntimeIdentifier passed via --runtime flag. Also added ARM64 to\nsupported platforms for .NET Core test project.\n\n* Add osx-arm64 runtime package dependency and RID graph entry\n\nThe main VowpalWabbit NuGet package was missing the osx-arm64 runtime\npackage in its dependencies, causing runtime failures when testing on\nARM64 macOS:\n\n  System.IO.FileNotFoundException: Could not load file or assembly\n  'VowpalWabbit.Core, Version=1.0.0.0'\n\nAdded osx-arm64 to:\n1. dotnetcore.nuspec.in dependencies (runtime package reference)\n2. runtime.json.in (RID graph for NuGet runtime resolution)\n\nThis allows .NET to correctly resolve and load the ARM64 native\nlibraries on macOS-14 runners.\n\n* Remove hardcoded x64 PlatformTarget from all .NET Core/Standard projects\n\nAll C# projects had PlatformTarget hardcoded to x64, causing\nBadImageFormatException when loading assemblies on ARM64:\n\n  System.BadImageFormatException: Could not load file or assembly\n  'VowpalWabbit.Core' - An attempt was made to load a program with\n  an incorrect format.\n\n.NET Standard/Core libraries should be built as AnyCPU (architecture-\nneutral MSIL), not architecture-specific. The native interop libraries\nare architecture-specific and loaded dynamically via P/Invoke based on\nthe runtime ID.\n\nRemoved <PlatformTarget>x64</PlatformTarget> from:\n- All library projects (vw.net.core, vw.common, vw.net, vw.json, etc.)\n- All executable projects (vw.console, simulator, unittest)\n\nThis allows the assemblies to run on any architecture (x64, ARM64, etc.)\nwith the appropriate native runtime package.",
