          "distinct": true,
          "id": "0562e684f392e3bfa6285e8cb93ba6f4938a51fb",
          "message": "fix: combined bugfixes for cover predict, LAS validation, shared model race, and search LDF oracle (#4838)\n\n* fix: remove redundant predict call in cb_explore_adf_cover learn path (#4274)\n\nThe condition controlling whether to call predict before learn in\ncover's predict_or_learn_impl was inverted. When base.learn_returns_prediction\nis true, cb_adf's learn functions (e.g., learn_mtr<true>) already include\nan internal predict call, making cover's explicit predict redundant.\n\nWhen base.learn_returns_prediction is false, cover was NOT calling predict,\nleaving predictions unpopulated for the algorithm that follows.\n\nFlip the condition from `if (base.learn_returns_prediction)` to\n`if (!base.learn_returns_prediction)` so that:\n- When learn returns predictions: skip redundant predict (fixes double-predict)\n- When learn does NOT return predictions: call predict to populate them\n\nFixes #4274\n\n* test: update cb_explore_adf cover audit reference output\n\nThe predict logic fix changes the audit output by removing redundant\nprediction lines that were produced by the incorrect condition.\n\n* fix: prevent --cover and --large_action_space from being used together (#4254)\n\nThe cover algorithm indexes its internal _action_probs array using action\nIDs directly. When large_action_space (LAS) filters the prediction set by\nerasing non-spanner actions, the remaining action IDs are non-contiguous\n(e.g., [0, 5, 12, 99]). Cover then tries to access _action_probs[99]\nwhen _action_probs only has 4 elements, causing a buffer overrun and\nnondeterministic AccessViolationException crashes.\n\nRather than adding complex action-index remapping throughout the cover\nalgorithm, add a validation check at setup time to prevent these\nincompatible options from being combined.\n\nFixes #4254\n\n* test: add test for --cover and --large_action_space incompatibility\n\nValidates that using --cover and --large_action_space together throws\nan error, which fixes the codecov/patch coverage gap.\n\n* fix: reorder disposal to prevent shared model race condition (#3782)\n\nThe seeded workspace shares weights (via shallow_copy/shared_ptr) and\nshared_data with the seed model. When disposing, both the old C++/CLI\nbinding (InternalDispose) and the new .NET binding (OperatorDelete)\nreleased the seed model reference BEFORE finishing/deleting the seeded\nworkspace. If that release dropped the last reference, the model would\nbe destroyed while the workspace still needed the shared resources,\ncausing an intermittent AccessViolationException.\n\nFix by reordering both bindings to finish/delete the workspace first,\nthen release the seed model reference. Also re-enable the TestSharedModel\ntest that was disabled due to this bug.\n\n* fix: correct LDF oracle generation in search module (#2175)\n\nThe C++ search reduction uses 0-based indices for oracle values and\npredictions in LDF mode, but uses 1-based action labels in non-LDF mode.\nThe Python oracle validation in pyvw.py incorrectly enforced `oracle > 0`\nfor both modes, preventing valid oracle=0 (first example) in LDF mode.\n\nA previous partial fix (afd570bb8) worked around this by shifting oracle\nvalues to 1-based in the Python examples, but this introduced an\noff-by-one error since the C++ code still compared oracle values against\n0-based indices in `allowed_actions_to_label()`.\n\nFix by:\n- Making the Python oracle assertion LDF-aware: allow oracle >= 0 for\n  LDF mode, keep oracle > 0 for non-LDF mode\n- Updating search_covington.ipynb CovingtonDepParserLDF to use correct\n  0-based oracle values and reverse mapping\n- Updating search_sequence_ldf.ipynb to use 0-based oracle (pos-1) and\n  convert predictions back to 1-based (pred+1)\n- Fixing search_speech_tagger.ipynb CovingtonDepParserLDF output mapping\n  (pred < n -> pred <= n) and re-enabling the LDF test\n- Fixing Python 2 -> 3 compatibility in WordAligner (has_key -> in)\n\nVerified: Covington LDF now produces [1, 2, -1, 4, 2] (correct) instead\nof [1, 2, -1, 1, 2] (broken). Sequence LDF produces [1, 2, 3, 1, 2].\nNon-LDF mode unaffected.",
          "timestamp": "2026-01-28T21:31:16-05:00",
