          "distinct": true,
          "id": "e3fb198e5be58ab80cfc295dcae8d7fe8bd3abee",
          "message": "ci: add flatbuffers test, static JNI build, extend retention (#4892)\n\n* docs: update CHANGELOG for Eigen 5.0.1 upgrade and C++14 minimum\n\n* fix: clean up pooled native examples on C# VowpalWabbit dispose (#4890)\n\nThe .NET bindings ported from C++/CLI left InternalDispose() and\nDisposeExample() as empty stubs, so pooled native examples were never\nfreed when a VowpalWabbit instance was disposed. This caused unbounded\nnative memory growth when creating/disposing many VW instances.\n\n- Add VowpalWabbitExample.DisposeNativeResources() to release the\n  native example handle and clear the InnerExample chain\n- Drain the example pool and free the trace listener GCHandle in\n  VowpalWabbitBase.OperatorDelete() before deleting the workspace\n- Fix ReturnExampleToPool to call DisposeNativeResources() instead of\n  Dispose() when the pool is full, avoiding infinite recursion\n- Fix LockFreeBagImpl.RemoveAll() to actually dequeue items instead of\n  returning the queue object itself\n- Add regression tests in TestLeak.cs exercising pool cleanup on dispose\n\nCloses #4890\n\n* ci: upgrade windows-2019 runners to windows-2022\n\nwindows-2019 GitHub Actions runners are deprecated and jobs queue\nforever waiting for a runner that never comes. Update all references:\n\n- native_nugets.yml: windows-2022 + \"Visual Studio 17 2022\" generator\n  (v141/v142 toolsets are still available on the windows-2022 image)\n- python_wheels.yml: windows-2022 runner and artifact names\n- build_windows_cmake.yml: drop windows-2019 from matrix\n- vendor_build.yml: drop windows-2019 from matrix\n\n* ci: use windows-latest for cmake and vendor builds\n\nThese workflows had a single-element matrix after dropping windows-2019.\nSwitch to windows-latest so they automatically pick up future runner\nimages.\n\n* ci: replace v141 toolset with v143 in native NuGet builds\n\nThe v141 (VS 2017) toolset is not installed on windows-2022 runners.\nDrop v141 and add v143 (VS 2022, the native toolset on windows-2022).\nKeep v142 (VS 2019) which is still available as an optional component.\n\nAdd nuget/native/test/test-v143.vcxproj for the NuGet test step.\n\n* ci: use latest Windows 10 SDK in NuGet test projects\n\nThe hardcoded SDK version 10.0.16299.0 is not installed on\nwindows-2022 runners. Use \"10.0\" to auto-select the latest\ninstalled SDK.\n\n* ci: add flatbuffers test, static JNI build, extend retention\n\n- Enable VW_FEAT_FLATBUFFERS in vcpkg-debug/vcpkg-release presets\n  and add flatbuffers test step to vcpkg_build.yml (Linux only),\n  closing the gap left by the stale Azure Pipeline build-linux.yml\n\n- Add static JNI build+test job to java-publish.yml, mirroring the\n  Azure Pipeline build-linux-java.yml (everything except deploy)\n\n- Extend artifact retention: native JNI libs 7→90 days, assembled\n  JARs 30→400 days (enterprise plan supports up to 400 days)\n\n* ci: set native JNI artifact retention to 400 days\n\nRelease artifacts should last as long as possible to avoid re-running\nthe pipeline unnecessarily.\n\n* ci: set 400-day retention for all release pipeline artifacts\n\nNuGet packages (native + .NET) and Python wheels/sdist now retain for\n400 days on pushes/tags, 7 days on PRs.\n\n* fix: resolve flatbuffer converter paths relative to ref_dir\n\nThe flatbuffer test converter used raw relative paths (e.g.\ntrain-sets/ref/0001.stderr) which only worked when CWD was the test/\ndirectory. Pass ref_dir through to FlatbufferTest so paths are\nresolved correctly regardless of working directory.\n\n* fix: run to_flatbuff subprocess with cwd=ref_dir\n\nThe to_flatbuff command contains many relative paths (e.g. -i\nmodel-sets/epsilon.model, -f models/...) that need to resolve\nrelative to the test/ directory. Set cwd=ref_dir on the subprocess\ncall to handle all relative paths at once.\n\n* fix: skip tests that fail flatbuffer conversion instead of aborting\n\nSome tests cannot be converted to flatbuffer format (e.g. certain\ndsjson/igl combinations). Instead of aborting the entire test run,\ncatch conversion errors and mark those tests as skipped.\n\n* fix: add newly-discovered tests to flatbuffer conversion skip list\n\nTests that produce different output in flatbuffer mode vs text/JSON\nmode (different warnings, float precision, etc.) need to be skipped\nin the automatic flatbuffer conversion test run. These tests were\nadded after the old Azure Pipeline stopped running.\n\n* fix: formatting and type annotation for flatbuffer converter changes\n\nRun black formatter and fix Optional[Path] type annotation to\nsatisfy pytype.\n\n* fix: use no_label for newline separators in flatbuffer converter\n\nThe to_flatbuff converter was creating typed labels (e.g. CS with empty\ncosts) for newline separator examples. When the FB parser read these back,\nit warned about empty label arrays and treated them as unlabeled, causing\ntest output differences.\n\nNow newline examples get no_label, matching text parser behavior. This\nfixes test 530 (csoaa with audit) and removes it from the FB skip list.\nAlso improves skip list comments for 146/148 and 298.\n\n* fix: remove tests 146/148 from flatbuffer skip list\n\nText and flatbuffer modes produce identical output on the same binary.\nThe -0.09 vs -0.08 difference was between the reference file (generated\nby CI's vcpkg build) and builds with different compiler flags, not a\nflatbuffer issue. Feature hashes are preserved exactly through the\nflatbuffer conversion.\n\n* style: apply clang-format to vw_to_flat.cc\n\n* fix: clarify flatbuffer skip list comments with accurate root causes\n\nGroup skipped tests by their actual failure reason rather than using\nvague catch-all descriptions.",
