          "message": "fix: thread-safe serializer cache and Key hash/equals consistency (#4919) (#4920)\n\n* fix: make VowpalWabbitSerializerFactory cache thread-safe (#4919)\n\nVowpalWabbitSerializerFactory.SerializerCache was a non-thread-safe\nDictionary<Key, object> shared across all callers and mutated without\nsynchronization. Concurrent construction of VowpalWabbit<TExample>\nwith a cold cache could throw InvalidOperationException from\nDictionary.TryInsert and corrupt the cache for subsequent calls.\n\nSwitch the cache to ConcurrentDictionary<Key, object>; both the\nTryGetValue read path and the indexer set path are thread-safe with\nno other code changes required.\n\nAdd a regression test that constructs VowpalWabbit<TExample> from\nten concurrent tasks.\n\n* fix: make Key.Equals/GetHashCode contract-consistent on CustomFeaturizer\n\nThe cache Key class compared CustomFeaturizer with SequenceEqual in Equals\nbut hashed it by reference in GetHashCode, violating the Equals/GetHashCode\ncontract. Two distinct list instances with identical content were treated\nas equal but produced different hashes, so they cached as separate entries\nand CreateSerializer redundantly recompiled the serializer.\n\nAlso fix a latent NullReferenceException / ArgumentNullException in Equals\nwhen exactly one CustomFeaturizer is null: the old code fell through to\nSequenceEqual and threw instead of returning false.\n\nHash CustomFeaturizer by iterating its contents, matching the SequenceEqual\nbehavior. Add a regression test asserting two settings with distinct-but-\nequal CustomFeaturizer lists return the same cached compiler instance.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: John Langford <jcl@microsoft.com>",
