          "distinct": true,
          "id": "8b9fadf7449206be86759d6ac0b6d6517bfe5a27",
          "message": "fix(ci): repair CI after the runner image upgrades (#4927)\n\n* fix(ci): quote CMAKE_POLICY_VERSION_MINIMUM so it survives shell parsing\n\nWindows CI jobs fail at the CMake Configure step with:\n\n  Invalid CMAKE_POLICY_VERSION_MINIMUM value \"3\".\n  A numeric major.minor[.patch[.tweak]] must be given.\n\neven though the workflows pass -DCMAKE_POLICY_VERSION_MINIMUM=3.5 on the\ncmake command line.\n\nRoot cause: on Windows runners the default shell for `run:` is PowerShell,\nwhich does not preserve the unquoted token `-DCMAKE_POLICY_VERSION_MINIMUM=3.5`.\nIt reaches cmake as two arguments -- `-DCMAKE_POLICY_VERSION_MINIMUM=3` plus a\nstray `.5` -- so the cache entry is set to the invalid value `3`. CMake reports\nthis in the same logs:\n\n  CMake Warning:\n    Ignoring extra path from command line:\n     \".5\"\n\nThis split is not new: the warning is present in previously *passing* runs too.\nCMake 3.31 tolerated the bare `3`, so the bug stayed latent. The windows-latest\nimage has since moved to CMake 4.x, which validates the value strictly and now\nfails configure outright.\n\nQuoting the argument makes it reach cmake as a single token. The quotes are\nstripped by both bash and PowerShell, so the change is a no-op for the\nLinux/macOS steps that share these lines.\n\nFixes configure for the Windows jobs in vendor_build, native_nugets,\ndotnet_nugets and codeql-analysis (csharp).\n\n* fix(ci): pin the .NET Framework jobs to windows-2022\n\nThe windows-latest image now ships Visual Studio 18 2026, which dropped both\nthe VS 2022 generator and the .NET Framework targeting packs. Once the\npolicy-version error is out of the way, configure gets further and these jobs\nfail at:\n\n  error MSB3644: The reference assemblies for .NETFramework,Version=v4.5.2\n  were not found.\n\nso windows-latest cannot build net452 at all, regardless of which generator is\nselected. Pin the two jobs that build .NET Framework to windows-2022 -- the\nimage they were passing on before windows-latest rolled forward -- rather than\nchasing the generator name.\n\nThis keeps the shipped package targeting net452. Retargeting to a newer\nframework would be a consumer-visible change and belongs in its own PR, as\ndoes eventually moving off windows-2022 before it is retired.\n\nnative_nugets.yml already pins windows-2022 for the same reason.\n\n* fix(ci): stop Homebrew's fmt from shadowing vcpkg's in the asan jobs\n\nasan.macos-15-intel has failed since June with:\n\n  vw/io/logger.h:166:28: error: no member named 'format' in namespace 'fmt'\n\neven though vcpkg installs a perfectly good fmt 12.1.0, and the identical\npreset passes on macos-14 and ubuntu-latest.\n\nRoot cause: `brew install ccache` pulls in Homebrew's fmt, because ccache\ndepends on it. On Intel runners Homebrew's prefix is /usr/local, whose include\ndirectory is on the compiler's default search path, so Homebrew's fmt headers\nshadow the ones vcpkg provides. Homebrew's fmt/core.h chains to base.h, which\ndeclares fmt::format_string but not fmt::format -- which is exactly why the\nlogger's signature resolves while the call in its body does not.\n\narm64 runners are unaffected because Homebrew installs into /opt/homebrew,\nwhich is not on the default include path. That asymmetry is why only\nmacos-15-intel breaks. Nothing in the repo changed; Homebrew's fmt rolled to a\nversion whose core.h no longer chains to format.h.\n\nVerified on a macos-15-intel runner, compiling the logger pattern against\nvcpkg's fmt 12.1.0:\n\n  before brew install ccache          -> compiles\n  after  brew install ccache          -> no member named 'format' in namespace 'fmt'\n  after  brew unlink fmt              -> compiles, ccache 4.13.6 still works\n\nUnlinking only removes the symlinks from /usr/local/include; ccache continues\nto resolve its own dylib through /usr/local/opt.",
