          "distinct": true,
          "id": "914e08990d2e055bcc7b8e5bcc614fec5e871aec",
          "message": "fix(ci): run the .NET Framework jobs on windows-latest again (#4928)\n\n#4927 pinned the .NET Framework jobs to windows-2022 as a stopgap, on the\ntheory that the newer image had dropped the .NET Framework targeting packs.\nThat theory was wrong, and the pin is worth retiring: windows-2022 will\neventually be retired by GitHub, and the pin holds two jobs on an older image\nfor no good reason.\n\nWhat actually happens: windows-latest ships targeting packs for v4.6.2 and\nnewer -- the supported ones. The two VW needs (v4.5.2, and v4.6 for the unit\ntests) went out of support in 2022, and the image leaves them behind as\ndirectories that are missing mscorlib.dll and RedistList/FrameworkList.xml.\nThe directories exist, so they look installed, but MSBuild resolves against\nthose two files and rejects the packs with MSB3644.\n\n  pack     windows-2022   windows-latest\n  v4.5.2   COMPLETE       incomplete      <- VW targets this\n  v4.6     COMPLETE       incomplete      <- unit tests target this\n  v4.6.2   COMPLETE       COMPLETE\n  v4.8.1   COMPLETE       COMPLETE\n\nInstalling the developer packs completes them, so unpin, and drop the\nhardcoded -G \"Visual Studio 17 2022\" while we are here: windows-latest ships\nVS 18 2026, and letting cmake pick the installed Visual Studio keeps this from\nbreaking again at the next image bump. The Windows job in vendor_build.yml\nalready works this way.\n\nVerified on a windows-latest runner:\n\n  targeting packs, before          v4.5.2 / v4.6 incomplete\n  configure (default generator)    ok, selects Visual Studio 18 2026\n  build .NET Framework             MSB3644\n  after installing the dev packs   v4.5.2 / v4.6 COMPLETE\n  build .NET Framework             ok\n\nThe dev pack install costs ~164s per job. This keeps the package targeting\nnet452, so nothing consumer-visible changes. Retargeting to v4.6.2 -- the\noldest framework still in support, and complete on both images -- would drop\nthe install and the dev-pack dependency, but it changes the shipped TFM\n(dotnet.nuspec.in derives it from CMAKE_DOTNET_TARGET_FRAMEWORK_VERSION), so\nit belongs in its own PR.\n\nnative_nugets.yml stays on windows-2022: it builds against the v142/v143\ntoolsets, which need VS 2022.",
