          "distinct": true,
          "id": "5801d8203d646b989180665753744ccc72d24065",
          "message": "fix: warn on invalid importance weights in all parsers (throw under --strict_parse) (#4926)\n\nA negative or NaN importance weight on a labeled example (e.g. `1 -1.0 | a b c`,\nor `\"Weight\": \"NaN\"` in JSON) was silently accepted and then dropped from training.\nThe scorer reduction's `ec.weight > 0` gate reroutes any example with weight <= 0 --\nand NaN, since `NaN > 0` is false -- to predict-only, so the model never trains on\nit and gd's documented `assert(ec.weight > 0.)` invariant is never exercised. The\nuser got no error, no warning, and exit code 0. This bit a user in production\n(#4925) where negative feedback signals encoded as negative importance weights were\nsilently ignored.\n\nReject negative and NaN importance weights in VW::setup_example -- the single point\nevery parsed example flows through -- so all input formats (text, JSON, CSV,\nflatbuffer, cache) and the library API behave identically. Follow VW's existing\nstrict_parse convention: log a warning by default (non-throwing, which matters for a\nlibrary) and throw a strict_parse_exception only under --strict_parse. A weight of\nexactly 0 is still accepted, preserving the documented predict-only / held-out\nconvention.\n\nThe check lives at example setup rather than in the scorer because the scorer is a\nshared choke point that cb_explore_adf --cover routes negative-weight examples\nthrough (cover drives example weights negative internally to diversify its cover\nset); setup_example runs at input time, before any reduction manipulates weights.\n\nFixes #4924\nFixes #4925",
          "timestamp": "2026-07-15T11:45:37-04:00",
          "tree_id": "a70454619336160d057ff9f537a8e9b709a6653b",
          "url": "https://github.com/VowpalWabbit/vowpal_wabbit/commit/5801d8203d646b989180665753744ccc72d24065"
