          "distinct": true,
          "id": "b660e3d06248581b5876be9671d4fa59d0e876e6",
          "message": "fix(ci): retry apt installs so a stalled mirror does not fail the job (#4936)\n\n* fix(ci): retry apt installs so a stalled mirror does not fail the job\n\nHosted Linux runners have been stalling inside `sudo apt-get` for hours at\na stretch. #4933 capped every job at 90 minutes so a stall costs 90 minutes\ninstead of six hours, but the job still fails, and re-running is a coin\nflip while the condition lasts.\n\nThe distinguishing property of this failure is that it is a hang, not an\nerror. `apt-get` never returns, so `-y`, `|| true` and Acquire::Retries\nare all irrelevant -- none of them can fire. Only a wall-clock bound\nconverts the hang into something a retry can act on.\n\nRoute every apt install through .github/scripts/apt-install.sh, which\nbounds each attempt with `timeout` and retries up to three times. A stalled\nattempt now costs five minutes and is retried, rather than consuming the\njob's whole budget. Failing all three attempts exits non-zero, so a genuine\npackaging problem still fails loudly instead of silently continuing without\nthe packages.\n\nThe one bare `apt-get update` with no install (python-docs) gets the same\nwall-clock bound inline; it is the step that hung for an hour earlier, and\nits trailing `|| true` could not help because the process never returned.\n\nTwo jobs called apt before checking out the repo, so they now do a\nsparse-checkout of .github/scripts first, rather than growing a second copy\nof the retry logic.\n\nThe wrapper's retry, exhaustion and no-argument paths were exercised\nlocally against a mock apt-get that hangs.\n\n* fix(ci): run apt timeouts as root and force-kill stuck processes\n\nThe first version of the wrapper ran `timeout` outside `sudo`:\n\n    timeout 300 sudo -E apt-get update ...\n\nThat cannot work. `timeout` then runs as the unprivileged runner user while\napt-get runs as root, so its SIGTERM is rejected with EPERM, and because no\n-k was given it went on waiting forever for a child that never died. The\nwrapper therefore reproduced the exact hang it was written to break, and the\nretry loop never got to iterate.\n\nObserved on this PR's own CI: lint.c++.formatting sat on the wrapped step\nfrom 03:35 to 05:05 and was killed by the 90 minute job cap, with\n`Terminate orphan process: pid (1835) (timeout)` in the cleanup -- the\ntimeout process still alive 88 minutes after its 300 second deadline.\n\nMove `timeout` inside sudo so it runs as root and can signal apt-get, and\nadd -k 30 so it escalates to SIGKILL when SIGTERM is ignored.\n\nVerified locally against a child that traps and ignores SIGTERM: the old\nform never returns, the new form returns rc=137 once the kill grace expires,\nand the full wrapper retries past two such hangs to exit 0.",
          "timestamp": "2026-08-18T07:00:08-04:00",
          "tree_id": "79f04282ba35dea25f500c4975e408273828fc89",
