          "distinct": true,
          "id": "2272a0104121b8a162604d3973dd4bc3e043cf64",
          "message": "ci: pin all GitHub Actions and container images to commit SHAs (#4932)\n\n* ci: pin all GitHub Actions and container images to commit SHAs\n\nzizmor's `unpinned-uses` (180 findings, ERROR) and `unpinned-images`\n(3 findings, ERROR) audits flag every workflow reference that resolves\nthrough a mutable pointer. A tag or branch can be repointed at arbitrary\ncode by whoever controls the upstream repository, so a compromised action\nruns with our workflow's token and secrets.\n\nEvery `uses:` is now pinned to a full commit SHA with a trailing version\ncomment, and the valgrind container is pinned by image digest. Each SHA\nwas resolved from the GitHub API and verified to be exactly the commit\nthe replaced tag points at -- no version changes, no behaviour changes.\n\nThe worst offenders were true moving targets rather than release tags:\n\n  lukka/get-cmake@latest                       -> v4.4.2\n  seanmiddleditch/gha-setup-ninja@master       -> v6\n  lukka/run-vcpkg@v11                          -> v11.6   (branch, not tag)\n  benchmark-action/github-action-benchmark@v1  -> v1.22.1 (branch, not tag)\n\n`vowpalwabbit/ubuntu2004-build:latest` is pinned to the digest it\ncurrently resolves to; that image has not been rebuilt since 2024-11-05,\nso the pin costs nothing today but must be bumped when it is.\n\nThe one pre-existing checkout pin in zizmor.yml is moved from v6.0.2 to\nthe same v6.1.0 SHA as everywhere else so the repository has a single\ncheckout pin to maintain.\n\nzizmor findings: 314 -> 131 (all 183 pinning errors cleared, none added).\n\n* ci: fix template injection and review the remaining workflow ERRORs (#4934)\n\nSecond of three PRs clearing zizmor's findings on .github/workflows.\nThis one is the semantic batch: everything that needed the workflow to be\nread rather than rewritten mechanically.\n\ntemplate-injection (19 findings)\n--------------------------------\nzizmor flags `env.*`, `steps.*.outputs.*` and `needs.*.outputs.*` when they\nare interpolated straight into a `run:` body, because the expansion happens\nbefore the shell parses the script -- a value containing shell metacharacters\nbecomes code. Both context families are reachable here: `env.VCPKG_ROOT` and\nfriends are rewritten at runtime through $GITHUB_ENV, and the version\noutputs come from ./utl/version_number.py, which a pull request can modify.\n\nSeventeen of these are now read from the environment instead of being\npasted in. No step's shell was changed: the Windows steps use PowerShell's\n`$env:NAME`, which is how the surrounding lines in these files already\nreference their environment, and the one cross-platform step converted\n(`Install package`) is a `cd`-and-`dotnet` script whose siblings already\ndeclare `shell: bash`.\n\nThe `Configure .NET Core` step is the exception. It runs on Linux, macOS and\nWindows, so no single env-var syntax works, and forcing `shell: bash` would\nchange how Windows parses its cmake arguments -- the exact failure #4927\nrepaired. Instead the version is validated where it is produced: every\n`Get version number` step now rejects anything outside the semver character\nset, so no shell metacharacter can reach any consumer of that output,\nincluding the ones zizmor does not flag (artifact names, package metadata).\nThat step carries a scoped ignore pointing at this reasoning.\n\ncache-poisoning (7 findings)\n----------------------------\nAll seven are the same false positive, and the audit's own docs explain why:\nit classifies a workflow as a publisher when a `push` branch filter contains\nthe string \"release\". VW's `releases/**` filter matches, so asan, vendor_build,\nwasm and python_checks are treated as publishing workflows.\n\nNone of them publish. asan uploads no artifact at all; vendor_build's only\nartifact is a vw binary with retention-days: 1 that a downstream test job\nconsumes; and the two setup-node findings are steps with no `cache:` input,\nso they restore nothing. The workflows that do produce VW's release\ndeliverables -- python_wheels, dotnet_nugets, native_nugets, java-publish --\ncontain no cache steps whatsoever, which is why zizmor never flagged them.\n\nVerified that no in-workflow remediation would satisfy the audit either:\nneither a step-level `if:` excluding release branches nor `lookup-only`\ngated on the branch changes the result, because the audit only inspects an\naction's cache-control input against tag-push expressions. Each site is\nsuppressed with the reasoning inline rather than restructured for a tool.\n\ndangerous-triggers (1 finding)\n------------------------------\ncheck_pr_title.yml uses pull_request_target, which is normally dangerous\nbecause it pairs a privileged token with untrusted head-ref code. This\nworkflow has no checkout and no `run:` steps -- the action reads the title\nfrom the event payload. The trigger is also load-bearing: it makes the\nworkflow definition come from the base branch, so a pull request cannot\ndisable its own title check by editing this file. Suppressed, with that\nreasoning recorded above the trigger.\n\nzizmor findings: 131 -> 104.\n\n* ci: make the cache-poisoning suppressions version-independent\n\nThe zizmor check on this PR reported 7 cache-poisoning errors that a local\nrun could not reproduce. The cause is a version split, not a disagreement\nabout the findings.\n\nzizmorcore/zizmor-action resolves its `version: latest` default from a\ndigest map baked into the action release, so the pinned v0.5.3 runs zizmor\n1.24.1 rather than the current 1.29.0. The two versions anchor a\ncache-poisoning finding differently: 1.29 reports it against the whole step,\nso a `# zizmor: ignore[...]` on the step's `name:` line covers it, while\n1.24 reports it against the `uses:` key alone and ignores a directive\nanywhere else in the step.\n\nMove the seven directives onto the `uses:` line, after the pin's version\ncomment. That placement is honoured by 1.24.1, 1.28.0 and 1.29.0 alike, so\nthe audit no longer depends on which zizmor the action happens to ship. The\nexplanatory comment above each step is unchanged, and the `# vX.Y.Z` pin\ncomment is preserved ahead of the directive.\n\nVerified against all three versions: 0 cache-poisoning and 0 errors in every\ncase. 1.24.1 -- what CI runs today -- now reports 103 findings, all of them\nthe excessive-permissions and artipacked work that #4935 clears.",
