          "distinct": true,
          "id": "872fd8da03677dbf83183890262a5d8f237ff3e0",
          "message": "ci: bump zizmor-action to v0.6.2 and name the zizmor version (#4941)\n\nThe audit has been running zizmor 1.24.1, five releases behind, without\nsaying so anywhere. zizmorcore/zizmor-action resolves its `version: latest`\ndefault from a digest map baked into the action release, so the zizmor\nversion is only ever as current as the action pin, and v0.5.3 shipped\n1.24.1.\n\nThat is not just staleness. The two versions anchor a cache-poisoning\nfinding differently -- 1.29 against the whole step, 1.24 against the `uses:`\nkey alone -- so a `# zizmor: ignore` comment that a local run accepted was\nsilently inert in CI, and the audit reported errors that could not be\nreproduced locally. Whichever version we run, local and CI have to agree.\n\nBump the action to v0.6.2 and state `version: 1.29.0` explicitly rather than\nleaving it implicit, so the audited version is visible in the workflow and\ncheckable against a local run. v0.6.2 lists 1.29.0 in its support/versions,\nso the pin resolves; an unknown version fails the step loudly rather than\nfalling back. The 0.6.x releases are version bumps plus an added `collect`\ninput, with no behaviour change for how we invoke the action.\n\nThis surfaces one new finding, `adhoc-packages` (Low) on the docs job's\n`pip install setuptools wheel` / `PyYAML` / `npm install -g handlebars`.\nIt is a true positive, not noise, so it is deliberately left visible rather\nthan suppressed: the audit's remediation is to install from a manifest that\nproduces a lockfile, and VW's requirements.txt and build-requirements.txt\nare unpinned today. Locking the docs dependencies is real work and belongs\nin its own change.\n\nVerified: 3dc1ecc is the v0.6.2 tag, 1.29.0 resolves from that release's\nversion map, and the tree audits clean under it apart from the one note.",
          "timestamp": "2026-08-18T09:53:39-04:00",
          "tree_id": "c0b30566055f103a890e0110a367e062f584155d",
